comments.body:
it's not about about changing as such, the feature doesn't exist yet, so there's nothing to preserve either.

MySQL doc that you're referring to does not say anything about OLD and NEW, they've introduced a nonstandard syntax that allows put a name after the {{VALUES (...)}} clause:
{code:sql}
INSERT INTO t1 (a,b,c) VALUES (1,2,3),(4,5,6) AS foo
  ON DUPLICATE KEY UPDATE c = foo.a+foo.b;
{code}
Besides being non-standard, it's not exactly clear, how to apply this to UPDATE RETURNING.

Oracle uses {{OLD}} and {{NEW}} keywords, like
{code:sql}
UPDATE t1 SET code= '2', description='22' WHERE id=2
RETURNING OLD code, OLD description, NEW code, NEW description;
{code}
It doesn't look like you can use it in expressions, so nothing like
{code:sql}
RETURNING OLD code + NEW code, CONCAT(OLD description, '/', NEW description);
{code}
Using {{OLD}}/{{NEW}} as in triggers is yet another option:
{code:sql}
UPDATE t1 SET code= '2', description='22' WHERE id=2
RETURNING OLD.code + NEW.code, CONCAT(OLD.description, '/', NEW.description);
{code}
this allows expressions all right. But 1) unclear how to do it for multi-table update. {{OLD.t1.code}} ? And unclear how to parse it inside a trigger, where {{OLD}} and {{NEW}} have well defined standard semantics.

{{VALUES()}} would allow to reuse existing feature, but it would provide the behavior opposite of what PostgreSQL has now. I'd rather avoid it, it would be unexpected for users.

The Description feels a bit outdated.
{code:sh}
git show mariadb-10.0.20:storage/innobase/include/univ.i|grep VERSION|head -3
{code}
{noformat}
#define INNODB_VERSION_MAJOR	5
#define INNODB_VERSION_MINOR	6
#define INNODB_VERSION_BUGFIX	25
{noformat}
MariaDB 10.0.20 claims to be based on the InnoDB from MySQL 5.6.25. That version includes {{Alter_inplace_info::RECREATE_TABLE}}, which had been added in MySQL 5.6.13 to support {{OPTIMIZE TABLE}} using the WL#6255 InnoDB online table rebuild algorithm whose original version was released as part of MySQL 5.6.8.

I think that the last missing piece to speed up data loading is MDEV-16281, to implement the multi-threaded creation of index trees.

bq. If you have two ListenStream= options, systemd will send in two separate socket file descriptors. Is that handled in the code?

Actually 3 since the unix socket is a ListenStream too. Up to 10 are handled within the code which is just a #define with non-fatal errors if this is exceeded and written to mysql error log.

