a



alter table t1 partition by key algorithm=base31 (a) partitions 3;
select * from t1 where a = ' ';
a



alter table t1 convert to CHARACTER SET cp1251 COLLATE cp1251_ukrainian_ci;
select * from t1 where a = ' ';
a

alter table t1 convert to CHARACTER SET utf8mb4 COLLATE utf8mb4_uca1400_ai_ci;
select * from t1 where a = ' ';
a



drop table t1;
{code}

This is because cs->strnxfrmlen(n) returns n for cp1251, so the second ALTER TABLE computes different hash values for the three strings. For utf8mb4 cs->strnxfrmlen(n) returns 16 for n=1, 2 or 3. But if the length of a string is much longer, then it fails utf8mb4 as well:

{code:c++}
create table t1 (a varchar(40));
insert into t1 values (' '), ('                               ');
select * from t1 where a = ' ';
a


alter table t1 partition by key algorithm=base31 (a) partitions 3;
select * from t1 where a = ' ';
a

alter table t1 convert to CHARACTER SET cp1251 COLLATE cp1251_ukrainian_ci;
select * from t1 where a = ' ';
a

alter table t1 convert to CHARACTER SET utf8mb4 COLLATE utf8mb4_uca1400_ai_ci;
select * from t1 where a = ' ';
a

drop table t1;
{code}

The docstring for my_strnxfrm_xxx() says:

{quote}
     Comparing two strings using memcmp() after my_strnxfrm_xxx()
     is equal to comparing two original strings with my_strnncollsp_xxx().

     Not more than 'dstlen' bytes are written into 'dst'.
     To guarantee that the whole string is transformed, 'dstlen' must be
     at least srclen*cs->strnxfrm_multiply bytes long. Otherwise,
     consequent memcmp() may return a non-accurate result.
{quote}

So the choice of dstlen is key for the main property to hold. It needs to be large enough. The fact that my_strnxfrmlen_* functions are needed means srclen * cs->strxfrm_multiply mentioned in the docstring may not be sufficient or optimal, and my_strnxfrmlen_simple suggests that cs->strxfrm_multiply may even be 0:

{code:c++}
size_t my_strnxfrmlen_simple(CHARSET_INFO *cs, size_t len)
{
  return len * (cs->strxfrm_multiply ? cs->strxfrm_multiply : 1);
}
{code}

I can think of two ideas:
1. Find a sufficiently large dstlen, but not too large to be wasteful. Maybe Field_* can pass pack_length() to Hasher::add as a maxlength and dstlen is then computed with cs->strnxfrmlen(maxlength)
2. Back to the previous idea of passing hash functions to the various my_hash_sort_* functions...

[~bar]: I implemented the idea of using pack_length(), with the assumption that pack_length() is a fixed number for a column independent of specific values in that column (is that right?), and pushed it as a new commit on top of the current one under review, ptal thanks:

{noformat}
fc448dc8b95 upstream/bb-main-mdev-9826-v3 MDEV-9826 [to-squash] Use strnxfrmlen(maxlen) instead of hardcoded 4096 for dstlen in strnxfrm
42bb4167b64 MDEV-9826 More hash algorithms for PARTITION BY [LINEAR] KEY
{noformat}

I'm asking to add a pure C level structure:
{code:cpp}
struct my_hasher_st
{
  void (*hash)(my_hasher_st *hasher, uchar value);
  ulong m_nr1;
  ulong m_nr2;
  /* We can add more members here, e.g. an uint32 member for crc32*/
};
{code}
where *{{hash}}* is a call-back function which will be called instead of *{{MY_HASH_ADD()}}*.

Also, possibly, two more call-back functions will be needed: to initialize *{{my_hasher_t}}* and to finalize it and return the hash value. But these are details already.

I'm inclined towards callback functions because:

- An implementation with strnxfrm (both streaming and non-streaming) will be slower than the one with callback functions.
- Additionally, the implementation with strnxfrm without streaming (the current implementation) is limited to a short buffer size. That means it won't be possible to reuse it for BLOB/TEXT unique keys.
- Implementing a streaming strnxfrm in a more complex task than callback functions. It'll contain a lot of almost-duplicate code and will make it harder to maintain the code.
- An implementation with callback functions will be elegant and easy to do. Most of changes will be mechanical.

