select partition_name, partition_method, partition_expression, partition_description, table_rows from information_schema.partitions where table_name='t1';
partition_name	partition_method	partition_expression	partition_description	table_rows
p0	RANGE COLUMNS	`c`	'2026-04-01'	0
p1	RANGE COLUMNS	`c`	'2026-05-02 00:00:00'	1
p2	RANGE COLUMNS	`c`	'2026-05-06 00:00:00'	1
drop table t1;
{code}

Next step will be the proper implementation in earnest: This implementation simply adds a new partition on top, with the range value which is the same as the current time. The actual implementation of course will create partitions by the specified interval. Test coverage needs to be improved too, including testing the "passthrough" behaviour.

This implementation uses the existing RANGE COLUMNS partitioning code path. The design doc TODO-5776 says that it should have its own partitioning type RANGE_INTERVAL_PARTITION for cleanliness. Another potential advantage is that it could support PARTITION BY RANGE (temporal_col) when INTERVAL is present. Currently PARTITION BY RANGE only allows an integer type partition expression, but we can extend it by using a new partition type RANGE_INTERVAL_PARTITION. In this case under the hood it should encode temporal data as an int, in range_int_array. It is more compatible with oracle, but the downside is that it could be potentially confusing: 1. VERSIONING_PARTITION has its own syntax PARTITION BY SYSTEM_TIME, whereas here we use the same syntax as PARTITION RANGE [COLUMNS]; 2. allowing PARTITION BY RANGE to have a temporal column is inconsistent with the existing PARTITION BY RANGE constraint. Perhaps we can defer the decision on this one.

