Using this comment to put together a specification.

*Background*. Some storage engines support row locks, such as innodb, aria, archive and rocksdb. 

Innodb use read/share (S) locks and write/exclusive (X) locks, where it uses table locks for locking a table, and row (or record) locks for locking individual rows. It also uses intention table locks IS/IX to signal intention to lock a row in a table, that is, an S (resp. X) row lock is acquired after an IS (or stronger than IS) (resp. IX) lock is acquired for the table. With MDEV-14479, no S (resp. X) row locks are acquired if the table is already locked with an S-or-stronger (resp. X) lock.

When are table locks acquired? Typically, LOCK TABLES ... WRITE should result in acquiring an X table lock, and presumably LOCK TABLES ... READ should result in an S table lock (TODO: check). And SELECT ... LOCK IN SHARE MODE (resp. FOR UPDATE) should set an IS (resp. IX) lock.

*This task*. It can be more efficient to use (actual, not intention) table locks than holding too many row locks. The task of the present issue is to implement an optimizer feature that signals to the storage engine when a table lock may be preferable, so that more statements (list below copied from the description) can benefit from MDEV-14479:

- {{CREATE ... SELECT}}
- {{INSERT ... SELECT}}
- {{SELECT ... LOCK IN SHARE MODE}}
- {{SELECT ... FOR UPDATE}}
- {{SELECT}} executed at {{TRANSACTION ISOLATION LEVEL SERIALIZABLE}}
- {{UPDATE}}
- {{DELETE}}

More specifically, as a first step, after store_lock and before reading any rows from the table, provide information about LIMIT to the storage engine when doing a full table/index scan without WHERE or JOIN conditions. An exception is when access happens during optimization, in which case the SQL layer does not have to signal the storage engine, with the assumption that such accesses do not read many rows anyway.

*References*
- https://mariadb.com/docs/server/server-usage/storage-engines/innodb/innodb-lock-modes
- Comments in storage/innobase/include/lock0priv.h starting from "/* LOCK COMPATIBILITY MATRIX"
- info:mysql-9.5#innodb-locking

