{code}


[~thiru] please feel free to take a look.

About *innodb.avoid_deadlock_with_blocked*, it is basically MDEV-34877, which effectively reorders the lock queue to avoid deadlocks. The failure is the second block in the test (with some trivial changes by me for debugging):

{code:sql}
--echo # The scenario when we bypass X<-S pair:
--echo # <con1, S, granted>,
--echo # <con1, S, granted>, <con2, X, waiting for con1>,
--echo # <con1, S, granted>, <con2, X, waiting for con1>, <con3, S, waiting for con2>
--echo # <con1, S, granted>, <con1, X, granted>, <con2, X, waiting for con1>, <con3, S, waiting for con2>

--connection con1
  BEGIN;
  /* con1 */ SELECT * FROM t1 LOCK IN SHARE MODE;  # S

--connection con2
  BEGIN;
  SET DEBUG_SYNC = 'lock_wait_before_suspend SIGNAL con2_will_wait';
  --send /* con2 */ SELECT * FROM t1 FOR UPDATE    # X

--connection con3
  SET DEBUG_SYNC = 'now WAIT_FOR con2_will_wait';
  BEGIN;
  SET DEBUG_SYNC = 'lock_wait_before_suspend SIGNAL con3_will_wait';
  --send /* con3 */ SELECT * FROM t1 LOCK IN SHARE MODE;   # S

--connection con1
  SET DEBUG_SYNC = 'now WAIT_FOR con3_will_wait';
  /* con1 */ SELECT * FROM t1 FOR UPDATE;  # X. FAILS ON WINDOWS with ER_LOCK_WAIT_TIMEOUT
  COMMIT;

--connection con2
if ($MTR_COMBINATION_ROW_LOCK) {
  --reap
}
if ($MTR_COMBINATION_TABLE_LOCK) {
  --error ER_LOCK_WAIT_TIMEOUT
  --reap
}
  COMMIT;

--connection con3
if ($MTR_COMBINATION_ROW_LOCK) {
  --reap
}
if ($MTR_COMBINATION_TABLE_LOCK) {
  --error ER_LOCK_DEADLOCK
  --reap
}
  COMMIT;
{code}

It only fails in the new "table lock mode" introduced in the current patch, and only on windows.

Locally on GNU/Linux, the test passes. In the "table lock mode", the con1 statement "SELECT * FROM t1 FOR UPDATE" causes a deadlock detection where the con3 transaction is marked as the victim and rolled back. It is not clear why no further deadlock detection was carried out given there is still a cycle <con1,X> -> <con2,X> -> <con1,S>. Instead the con2 transaction times out. It is also not clear what happens in Windows: why does the con1 transaction time out when the deadlock is formed?

If we set global innodb_print_all_deadlocks= 1, the deadlock log looks more or less identical between buildbot Windows (https://ci.mariadb.org/64647/logs/amd64-windows/nm/mysqld.1.err.19) and local GNU/Linux runs. So the deadlock detection process is the same but somehow the con1 transaction times out. Perhaps it is expected if either the con1 transaction or the con2 transaction times out?

I noticed some other tests with nondeterministic error expectations "\-\-error 0,ER_LOCK_WAIT_TIMEOUT" (see e.g. MDEV-13262) so perhaps there's a race condition here too and we could fix this in the same way? Pushed a new commit on top.

The behaviour discrepancy between "row lock mode" (lock queue reordering) and "table lock mode" (deadlock/timeout) suggests MDEV-34877 only applies to row locks, presumably because table locks are not widely used before the present task.

With *innodb.insert_into_empty* run with ps protocol, the statement "INSERT INTO t1 VALUES (0,1)" does not get executed, because the preparation fails checks. Without a failing bulk insertion that would cause the rollback of the previous INSERT statement, the three rows survive and we get ER_DUP_ENTRY. So it can be fixed by disabling ps protocol say for the (0,1) insertion.

{code:sql}
BEGIN;
SELECT * FROM t1 LOCK IN SHARE MODE;
INSERT INTO t1 VALUES (0),(1),(2);
--error ER_WRONG_VALUE_COUNT_ON_ROW
INSERT INTO t1 VALUES (0,1);
if ($MTR_COMBINATION_ROW_LOCK) {
  --error ER_DUP_ENTRY
  INSERT INTO t1 VALUES (2);
}
if ($MTR_COMBINATION_TABLE_LOCK) {
  INSERT INTO t1 VALUES (2);
}
COMMIT;
{code}

