{quote}
So if the binlog contains:

0-1-10 0-1-11 0-2-10 0-2-11 0-2-12 0-3-11 0-3-12

Then a starting position of 0-2-10 must start with the GTID 0-2-11, not 0-1-11.
Likewise, a stop postion of 0-3-11 must stop at 0-3-11, not 0-1-11 or 0-2-11.
{quote}

That seems a bit arbitrary. Say we have a (slightly broken) switchover with 

{noformat}
0-2-10 0-1-10 0-1-11 0-1-12 ... (only 0-1-... from here on out)
{noformat}

and a start position of 0-2-10. There would be no 0-2-11 to find, and all future transactions would be skipped. Though generally, I suppose it doesn't matter whether or not the logic is arbitrary or not, {{mysqlbinlog}} should behave the same as a slave. I.e. if the slave provides some GTID to start replication, {{mysqlbinlog}} should output the same transactions.

Also as [~ParadoxV5] points out, it also only affects the {{--skip-gtid-strict-mode}} case. Perhaps warning about a missed {{--stop-position}} with {{--skip-gtid-strict-mode}} doesn't make much sense logically anyway. It would make sense to me to make the warning for {{--gtid-strict-mode}} only.  _Though this doesn't help the {{--start-position}} case. That'd be a bug to fix either way._

Update: {{--start-position}} is also inconsistent and wrong.
{{mariadb-binlog}} starts after 0-1-2 with --start-position=0-9-2, whereas the IO thread fatal errors with:
{code:sql}
SET @@GLOBAL.gtid_slave_pos= '0-9-1';
CHANGE MASTER TO master_use_gtid=slave_pos;
{code}
({{ER_MASTER_FATAL_ERROR_READING_BINLOG}}: …which is not in the master's binlog. …)

----

ICYMI:
This inconsistency is the root cause to “No warning with Server ID not found“; fixing this cause will naturally solve that effect.
P.S. Don’t worry about code compatibility; I’m certain it is adaptable.

