- source build, no packages
- feature-complete debug build:
{noformat}
cmake . -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Debug -DWITH_EMBEDDED_SERVER=1 -DHAVE_EMBEDDED_PRIVILEGE_CONTROL=1 -DWITH_DBUG_TRACE=OFF
{noformat}
with all compression providers, engines which agree to build on 32-bit, etc.

Tests:

Note: everywhere in this text "default test set" means that no tests or suites are listed on the command line, either as included or excluded, MTR collects tests and suites itself.

- "Normal": default test set
- "Hashicorp": hashicorp plugin tests with --big
{noformat}
--suite=vault --parallel=1 --big
{noformat}
It needs the hashicorp vault to be installed and configured. The old buildbot does it dynamically, the script is in the repository. Alternatively it can be pre-installed, but still some configuration will be needed every time -- opening the vault etc.
In the old bb, takes a couple minutes, including configuration.
Better not to run in parallel.
- "Extra": some non-default suites with "big"
{noformat}
--suite=jp,spider,spider/bg,engines/funcs,engines/iuds --big
{noformat}
can be run in parallel, but keep in mind that spider is very demanding in terms of resources, it can run many servers at once
- "Optimizer trace": main suite with optimizer trace enabled
{noformat}
--suite=main --mysqld=--optimizer_trace=enabled=on
{noformat}
- "Embedded": default test set with the embedded server 
{noformat}
--embedded-server
{noformat}
- "PS protocol": default test set with the PS protocol
{noformat}
--ps-protocol
{noformat}
- "View protocol": main test suite with the view protocol
{noformat}
--view-protocol --suite=main
{noformat}
- "PS embedded": default test set with the embedded server and PS protocol
{noformat}
--ps --embedded
{noformat}

It would also be good to add S3 suite here (e.g. to "Extra" step), otherwise there will be a separate task for it.


Steps can be recombined for optimization, as long as nothing is lost. They can also be run as two or more separate builders if you so prefer, currently in the old bb they are split into two.

Depending on how long they end up to be, we can later add more test steps there, but these must be present. 

Please don't name it "fulltest2", make it more sensible, like x86-fulltest, or x86-debug-fulltest, or x86-debian-12-fulltest-debug, whatever.

comments.body:
[~danblack],
{noformat}
         IS_DEPRECATED: 0
{noformat}
this is rather unconventional, I don't remember many "boolean" (integer 0/1) columns in I_S. Usually it's YES/NO. See {{READ_ONLY}} in the same table.

But I'd prefer to have only one column, I don't like redundancy. May be {{DEPRECATED_REPLACEMENT}} being NULL means not deprecated, empty string means no replacement. That would be tricky in Oracle mode though. "NO" means no replacement? Or, may be two columns are fine, I don't like redundancy, but SQL Standard has lots of it.

[~Roel], no, my point was that the original query was supposed to show only changed values. It was intentionally excluding {{COMPILE-TIME}}. You added {{COMPILE-TIME}} in your query and then noted that it shows everything. It doesn't mean the original query was wrong, it was correct and was working as advertised.

The query
{code:sql}
SELECT VARIABLE_NAME, GLOBAL_VALUE, DEFAULT_VALUE
  FROM INFORMATION_SCHEMA.SYSTEM_VARIABLES
  WHERE GLOBAL_VALUE_ORIGIN IN('CONFIG', 'SQL');
{code}
shows all modified variables. As the description says
bq. The new thing here is {{IS_DEPRECATED='YES'.}}

For plugins, if {{PLUGIN_VAR_DEPRECATED}} and {{DEPRECATED_REPLACEMENT}} are set, will {{DEPRECATED_REPLACEMENT}} ever show? It seems the plugin interface has no field for a replacement name and thus {{DEPRECATED_REPLACEMENT}} would always show as NULL. Perhaps we can document this as a known limitation: 
{noformat}
For variables declared by plugins, the DEPRECATED_REPLACEMENT column is always NULL; only server-declared variables can show a possible replacement.
{noformat}

For a bit of extra code coverage, we could decide to add this fairly-maintenance free query (always returs 1 if there is at least one {{DEPRECATED_REPLACEMENT}} value) to one of the tests:
{code:sql}
  SELECT COUNT(*) > 0 AS result                                                                     
  FROM INFORMATION_SCHEMA.SYSTEM_VARIABLES                                                                                
  WHERE IS_DEPRECATED='YES' AND DEPRECATED_REPLACEMENT IS NOT NULL;
{code}
{noformat:title=MDEV-35369 CS 13.0.0 cb6cebe4ed0630beed9f18f3c3e0ae14847434cf (Debug, Clang 21.1.3-20250923) Build 01/04/2026}
13.0.0-dbg>  SELECT COUNT(*) > 0 AS result                                                                     
    ->   FROM INFORMATION_SCHEMA.SYSTEM_VARIABLES                                                                                
    ->   WHERE IS_DEPRECATED='YES' AND DEPRECATED_REPLACEMENT IS NOT NULL;
+--------+
| result |
+--------+
|      1 |
+--------+
1 row in set (0.022 sec)
{noformat}

