[~serg] For these packages here are the differences compared to what we released previously:

*11.8 (compared to 11.8 preview)*
{code}
ERROR: We have extra files! {'lib/galera/libgalera_smm.so'}
ERROR: We have missing files from the archive! {'bin/garbd'}
ERROR: We have extra deps for lib/libgalera_smm.so! {'libcrypto.so.1.1', 'libssl.so.1.1'}
ERROR: We have missing deps for lib/libgalera_smm.so! {'libssl.so.1.0.0', 'libcrypto.so.1.0.0'}
ERROR: We have extra deps for bin/mariadb! {'libtinfo.so.6'}
ERROR: We have missing deps for lib/plugin/auth_gssapi.so! {'libpthread.so.0'}
{code}
*11.7 (compared to 11.7.1)*
{code}
ERROR: We have extra files! {'lib/galera/libgalera_smm.so', 'lib/libgalera_smm.so'}
ERROR: We have extra deps for bin/mariadb! {'libtinfo.so.6'}
ERROR: We have missing deps for lib/plugin/auth_gssapi.so! {'libpthread.so.0'}
{code}

Is this acceptable?

Is listed twice because of DEXTRA_FILES

{code:java}
-DEXTRA_FILES="${WSREP_PROVIDER}=lib;${WSREP_PROVIDER}=lib/galera;" \
{code}

This was taken from old-bb code which got commented at some point
https://github.com/MariaDB/mariadb.org-tools/blob/b92c4e5db110dbfed22894ef0980f09a31c134f5/buildbot/maria-master.cfg#L5323


{code:java}
#      extra="-DEXTRA_FILES=$p/lib/libgalera_smm.so=lib;$p/lib/galera/libgalera_smm.so=lib/galera"
{code}

So I guess we started to manually add it into the packages since this commit? https://github.com/MariaDB/mariadb.org-tools/commit/90f5da9f8a09e75695859845800ae4e939852a9d

Should be installed now:


{code:java}
[root@hz-freebsd-bbw2 /usr/ports/databases/galera26]# /usr/local/bin/garbd  --version
 INFO: 26.4.21.rmariadb-26.4.21
{code}

I mentioned it earlier in MDBF-866:
{quote}
11.7 does not build on the centos-7 bintar builder anymore either, so maybe it's just easier to switch to the new one instead of keeping fixing the old one.
{quote}
Sergei agreed there, so I guess there is no need to wait and see whether it gets fixed, just switch 11.7 to the new builder.

[~serg] I've checked the bintars with the script here:

https://github.com/MariaDB/buildbot/pull/694

The results are as follows:

10.6, 10.11. 11.4 all have the following differences compared to *our previously released bintars (version by version)*:
{code}
ERROR: We have missing files from the archive! {'lib/libgalera_smm.so', 'bin/garbd'}
ERROR: We have extra deps for bin/mariadb! {'libtinfo.so.5'}
ERROR: We have missing deps for lib/plugin/auth_gssapi.so! {'libpthread.so.0'}
{code}

Missing files -> Files not in archive compared to previous release
Extra -> The archive has more deps than previous release
Missing -> The archive has fewer deps than previous release
Is this acceptable?

[~serg]
ncurses is linked statically in old buildbot, new buildbot too.

the only difference between the two, is that in old buildbot , ncurses is compiled statically slightly differently, some functions are part of libtinfo.a and there is a bug in cmake / readline.cmake where it will not respect CMAKE_LIBRARY_PATH to search for static libtinfo. 

Old bb ncurses static variant has libtinfo embedded in libncurses.a
I tested today with [~cvicentiu] and we will proceed with compiling ncurses statically same as in old buildbot.
The updates will be applied to both new bintar (alma linux8) and centos-7-bintar and this error will no longer be present: 
{code:java}
bin/mariadb! {'libtinfo.so.5'}
{code}

As for 
{quote}
exclude from analysis the debuginfo packages
{quote}
Sure, I have no objections to that, I thought we were already trying to do it, but apparently it was more fine-grained than it needed to be (e.g. we were trying to skip build-id, or something). I don't think we need to worry about debuginfo dependencies at all.

I would however be interested to know if somebody has any insights about the difference above ([~danblack] maybe?), but it shouldn't be an obstacle to anything.

MySQL supports index hints inside views.
{code:sql}
CREATE VIEW v1 AS SELECT /*+ NO_INDEX(t1) */ a FROM t1 WHERE b IN
   (SELECT /*+ NO_INDEX(t1) */ a FROM t1 WHERE a > 3) ORDER BY a;

SHOW CREATE VIEW v1;

View	Create View	character_set_client	collation_connection
v1	CREATE ALGORITHM=UNDEFINED DEFINER=`root`@`localhost` SQL SECURITY DEFINER VIEW `v1` AS select /*+ NO_INDEX(`t1`@`select#1`) NO_INDEX(`t1`@`select#2`) */ `t1`.`a` AS `a` from `t1` where `t1`.`b` in (select `t1`.`a` from `t1` where (`t1`.`a` > 3)) order by `t1`.`a`	utf8mb4	utf8mb4_0900_ai_ci
{code}

However, we have another numeration of query blocks in MariaDB
{code:sql}
CREATE VIEW v1 AS SELECT /*+ NO_INDEX(t1) */ a FROM t1 WHERE b IN
   (SELECT /*+ NO_INDEX(t1) */ a FROM t1 WHERE a > 3) ORDER BY a;

select * from information_schema.views where table_name='v1';
TABLE_CATALOG   TABLE_SCHEMA    TABLE_NAME      VIEW_DEFINITION CHECK_OPTION    IS_UPDATABLE    DEFINER SECURITY_
TYPE    CHARACTER_SET_CLIENT    COLLATION_CONNECTION    ALGORITHM
def     test    v1      select /*+ NO_INDEX(`t1`@`select#3`) NO_INDEX(`t1`@`select#2`) */ `test`.`t1`.`a` AS `a` 
from `test`.`t1` where `test`.`t1`.`b` in (select `test`.`t1`.`a` from `test`.`t1` where `test`.`t1`.`a` > 3) ord
er by `test`.`t1`.`a`   NONE    YES     root@localhost  DEFINER latin1  latin1_swedish_ci       UNDEFINED
Warnings:
Warning 4218    Query block name `select#3` is not found for NO_INDEX hint
{code}

However, looking at the following query execution by MySQL
{code:sql}
EXPLAIN  SELECT /*+ INDEX(ta i_a) */ ta.a FROM v1, t1 ta WHERE ta.a > 3;
id	select_type	table	partitions	type	possible_keys	key	key_len	ref	rows	filteredExtra
1	SIMPLE	<subquery3>	NULL	ALL	NULL	NULL	NULL	NULL	NULL	100.00	NULL
1	SIMPLE	t1	NULL	ALL	NULL	NULL	NULL	NULL	256	12.50	Using where; Using join buffer (hash join)
1	SIMPLE	ta	NULL	range	i_a	i_a	5	NULL	160	100.00	Using where; Using index; Using join buffer (hash join)
3	MATERIALIZED	t1	NULL	ALL	NULL	NULL	NULL	NULL	256	33.33	Using where
Warnings:
Note	1003	/* select#1 */ select /*+ INDEX(`ta`@`select#1` `i_a`) */ `test`.`ta`.`a` AS `a` from `test`.`t1` /*+ NO_INDEX(`t1`@`select#1`) */  semi join (`test`.`t1` /*+ NO_INDEX(`t1`@`select#2`) */ ) join `test`.`t1` `ta` where ((`test`.`t1`.`b` = `<subquery3>`.`a`) and (`test`.`ta`.`a` > 3) and (`test`.`t1`.`a` > 3))
{code}

we can see the view definition merged into the top-level select and the query block numbers (`select#1`, `select#2`) are intermingled:
{code:sql}
/* select#1 */ select /*+ INDEX(`ta`@`select#1` `i_a`) */ `test`.`ta`.`a` AS `a` 
     from `test`.`t1` /*+ NO_INDEX(`t1`@`select#1`) */  
                 semi join (`test`.`t1` /*+ NO_INDEX(`t1`@`select#2`) */ ) join `test`.`t1` `ta` 
     where ((`test`.`t1`.`b` = `<subquery3>`.`a`) and (`test`.`ta`.`a` > 3) and (`test`.`t1`.`a` > 3))
{code}

Probably, this can be solved by generating explicit query block names inside views:
{code:sql}
CREATE VIEW v1 AS 
SELECT /*+ QB_NAME(qb1) NO_INDEX(t1@qb1) */ a 
FROM t1 WHERE b IN
   (SELECT /*+ QB_NAME(qb2) NO_INDEX(t2@qb2) */ a FROM t1 WHERE a > 3) 
ORDER BY a;
{code}

Anyway, it looks like supporting hints in views is a more complex task (some complications are mentioned at the description of MDEV-33281), and it was decided not to introduce this support now.

{quote}
SLES 15 SP6 (from new bb) doesn't have libpcre2-posix2 , instead only libpcre2-posix3 is available.
Mainly, MariaDB-test package built by buildbot.mariadb.net cannot be installed on SLES 15 SP6.
{quote}
I don't think it's related to buildbots' differences at all, we see the same problem on openSUSE 15.5 vs 15.6 in the old BB.
It is a SLES versioning problem which was discussed before: if packages from older 15.x *could* be installed on 15.6, we could, in theory, build only on 15.x and release for all SLES 15. Otherwise they must be released separately. The old BB builds packages on 15.3, obviously as the above demonstrates they are not installable on 15.6, so packages for 15.6 must be built on 15.6 and released from there, and minor tests between 15.\[0-5\] => 15.6 packages are meaningless as we treat it as a major system upgrade.

Whether there is any need to keep releasing old SLES 15.x (for the sake of 10.5/10.6) is another question which AFAIR is being discussed by Sergei and Daniel Black. Personally, I'm happy with "the less various packages for SUSE the better" approach, although I feel for the users whose upgrade paths are rather unclear.

{quote}
For Minor (ColumnStore)
{quote}
Same observation as above, apparently there is no point in trying to install older 15.x SLES packages on 15.6. Again, we see the same problem with openSUSE 15.5/15.6 packages in the old BB. Quite possibly this problem will disappear automatically when the general SLES dilemma is solved.
On top of the sentiment above about SUSE being problematic for packaging and upgrade, it can be noted that Columnstore so far never cared much for SUSE, they declared it as unsupported system more than once, I doubt they even have SUSE builders in their drones, and complaints raised before didn't get high priority, so I think releasing Columnstore for SUSE officially is pushing our luck. If we don't release any 11.4 Columnstore packages for SUSE and users didn't complain so far, maybe it's the way to go.

