An example of interesting behaviour when an index hint forbids using an index as a rowid filter.

Setup:
{code:sql}
CREATE TABLE t1 (a INT, b VARCHAR(10), c VARCHAR(1024), KEY key_a(a), KEY key_b(b), KEY key_c(c));

INSERT INTO t1 VALUES
  (1,'w','z'), (1,'X','o'), (1,'q','c'), (5,'w','c'), (2,'j','m'),
  (2,'Q','s'), (9,'e','J'), (2,'p','W'), (9,'o','F'), (2,'g','S'),
  (1,'Y','a'), (NULL,'Y','p'), (NULL,'s','x'), (NULL,'i','S'),
  (1,'l','q'), (7,'r','e'), (4,'b','h'), (NULL,'E','c'),
  (NULL,'M','a'), (3,'e','X'), (NULL,'p','r'), (9,'e','i'),
  (3,'g','x'), (2,'h','y');

ANALYZE TABLE t1;
{code}

See that rowid filter is applied by default (without hints). 
{code:sql}
EXPLAIN EXTENDED
SELECT a FROM t1
  WHERE c < 'e' AND b > 't' ORDER BY a;

id	select_type	table	type	possible_keys	key	key_len	ref	rows	filtered	Extra
1	SIMPLE	t1	range|filter	key_b,key_c	key_b|key_c	43|1003	NULL	5 (25%)	25.00	Using index condition; Using where; Using filesort; Using rowid filter
{code}

`key_b` is used in conjunction with `key_c` which is applied as a rowid filter, OK.

However, when a FORCE INDEX/USE INDEX hint forcing the use of the same `key_b` is specified then `key_c` is not applied as a rowid filter any more:
{code:sql}
EXPLAIN EXTENDED
SELECT a FROM t1 FORCE INDEX(key_b)
WHERE c < 'e' AND b > 't' ORDER BY a;
id	select_type	table	type	possible_keys	key	key_len	ref	rows	filtered	Extra
1	SIMPLE	t1	range	key_b	key_b	43	NULL	5	20.83	Using index condition; Using where; Using filesort
{code}

On the other hand, if the hint includes `key_c`: FORCE INDEX(key_b, key_c) / USE INDEX(key_b, key_c) then the rowid filter `key_c` is applied as expected. So, effectively the hints white-list both the indexes which can be used for data access and for building rowid filters.

This behavior is reproducible on versions before 12.0 (when new-style hints were introduced), so it's not a recent regression. 

I am not sure whether it is a bug or expected behavior, but it would be nice to understand that so I can write relevant test cases for the new ROWID_FILTER, NO_ROWID_FILTER hints.

There is a challenge with scenario like this: one wants to access the table by `key_d` with rowid filter built from `key_b`
{code:sql}
SELECT /*+ index(t1 key_d) rowid_filter(t1 key_b)*/ a FROM t1
  WHERE c < 'e' AND b > 't' and d < 'e';
{code}

However, the index(t1 key_d) hint excludes all other indexes except `key_d` from the consideration, so `key_b` is not applied as rowid filter. To be considerable, `key_b` has to be present in TABLE::opt_range_keys map:
{code}
void TABLE::init_cost_info_for_usable_range_rowid_filters(THD *thd)
{
  ...
  key_map::Iterator it(opt_range_keys);
{code}

This map is composed at check_quick_select() call
{code:title=check_quick_select()}
param->table->opt_range_keys.set_bit(keynr);
{code}
and the key must be present at `JOIN_TAB::const_keys` to make it into the `opt_range_keys` map. `const_keys` are formed by filtering possible keys with the `keys_in_use_for_query` bitmap (which is composed in accordance to the INDEX() hint). 

Relaxing `keys_in_use_for_query` filter by adding `key_b` into it leads to the opposite effect: `key_b` becomes available not only for rowid filtering by also for data retrieval which is not desired.
The logic of choosing indexes is quite complicated, and attempts to implement new bitmaps like `keys_in_use_for_rowid_filter` or `keys_in_use_for_query_and rowid_filter` are not yet successful as undesirable side effects are still present.

[~oleg.smirnov], what about this example: key 'key_c' is not listed in FORCE INDEX(key_b), but is still used

{code:sql}
EXPLAIN format=json
SELECT /*+ rowid_filter(t1 key_c)*/ a FROM t1  FORCE INDEX(key_b)
  WHERE c < 'e' AND b > 't' and d < 'e';
{code}

{code:sql}
EXPLAIN  format=json 
SELECT /*+ rowid_filter(t1 key_c)*/ a FROM t1  FORCE INDEX(key_b)
WHERE c < 'e' AND b > 't' and d < 'e';
EXPLAIN
{
  "query_block": {
    "select_id": 1,
    "cost": 0.004537718,
    "nested_loop": [
      {
        "table": {
          "table_name": "t1",
          "access_type": "range",
          "possible_keys": ["key_b", "key_c", "key_d"],
          "key": "key_d",
          "key_length": "803",
          "used_key_parts": ["d"],
          "rowid_filter": {
            "range": {
              "key": "key_c",
              "used_key_parts": ["c"]
            },
            "rows": 5,
            "selectivity_pct": 20.83333333
          },
          "loops": 1,
          "rows": 4,
          "cost": 0.004537718,
          "filtered": 4.340277672,
          "index_condition": "t1.d < 'e'",
          "attached_condition": "t1.c < 'e' and t1.b > 't'"
        }
      }
    ]
  }
}
{code}

items.toString:
Add a new-style hint to control Rowid Filters.

{code:sql}
/* +ROWID_FILTER( [table_name [index_name [ ...] ]]) */
/* +NO_ROWID_FILTER([table_name [index_name [ ... ] ]] ) */
{code}

h2. Hint applicability 

Like {{NO_RANGE_OPTIMIZATION}} or {{MRR}}, the hint can apply to
* Query block  - {{NO_ROWID_FILTER()}} 
* Table  - {{NO_ROWID_FILTER(table_name)}}
* Specific index(es)  - {{NO_ROWID_FILTER(table_name index1 index2 ...}} 

h2. Semantics

h3. Semantics of NO_ROWID_FILTER 

Do not consider Rowid Filter for the scope of the hint (all tables in the query block, specific table, specific index(es)).

h2. Semantics of ROWID_FILTER

In short: Force use of ROWID_FILTER for the {{table.index}} it targets (?)

TODO: 

- When creating potential rowid filters, only consider the specified indexes.  

- the ROWID_FILTER hint doesn't affect the choice of access method.
  If the optimizer picks an index for which this rowid filter can be applied, good. If not, not our problem.

- When considering whether to use the rowid filter together with an index, 
  make a cost-based choice between specified filters.

There are some ideas about specifying the buffer size available for the rowid filter but they are outside of the scope of this MDEV.

