It shows original_condition from both selects without telling which is which (not perfect)  but at least it doesn't show equality_propagation steps.

I believe we're supposed to generate warnings about wrong derived tables names?
{code:sql}
--source include/have_sequence.inc

create table t1 select seq as i, 10*seq as j from seq_1_to_10;
create view v1 as select * from t1;
set session optimizer_switch='derived_merge=off';

explain extended
  SELECT /*+ merge(wrong_name) */a, b FROM
     (SELECT /*+ merge(wrong_name) */ i as a, j as b FROM 
        (select i*10 as i, j*5 as j from v1) dt_in) AS dt_out
  WHERE a < 3 AND b > 8;
{code}
For some reason I can't see any warning here.

Looking at this piece of code:
{code:cpp}
void Opt_hints_qb::fix_hints_for_table(TABLE_LIST *table_list)
{
  Opt_hints_table *tab= static_cast<Opt_hints_table *>(find_by_name(table_list->alias));
...
  if (is_fixed())
  {
    if (tab && !tab->is_fixed())
    {
...
      table_list->opt_hints_qb= this;
      table_list->opt_hints_table= tab;
==>      set_fixed(Opt_hints::Fixed_state::DELAYED);
    }
...
{code}
Are you setting this `delayed` state for the whole query block? Shouldn't it be set at the table level? There may be more than one derived table in a query block and I'm not sure the logic will handle it correctly.
For example, query
{code:sql}
explain extended SELECT /*+ NO_MERGE(dt) NO_MERGE(wrong_name)*/ * FROM
  (SELECT i as a, j as b FROM t1) AS dt, (SELECT i as a, j as b FROM t1) AS dt2
    WHERE dt.a = dt2.a and dt.b = dt2.b AND dt.a < 3 AND dt.b > 8;
{code}
does not produce warning about missing `wrong_name` table.

I am also thinking whether we can always fix table-level hints against `TABLE_LIST` instead of `TABLE`.  One obvious problem is we need `TABLE::key_info` to resolve index-level hints and it's not clear how to get to it from `TABLE_LIST`...

Let me clarify what I was trying to say in my last message, and let's look the example below (which doesn't work correctly: merges one of DTs despite there are hints NO_MERGE for both). 
{code:sql}
explain extended SELECT /*+ NO_MERGE(dt2) NO_MERGE(dt)*/ * FROM
(SELECT i as a, j as b FROM t1) AS dt, (SELECT i as a, j as b FROM t1) AS dt2
WHERE dt.a = dt2.a and dt.b = dt2.b AND dt.a < 3 AND dt.b > 8;
id	select_type	table	type	possible_keys	key	key_len	ref	rows	filtered	Extra
1	PRIMARY	t1	ALL	NULL	NULL	NULL	NULL	10	100.00	Using where
1	PRIMARY	<derived2>	ref	key0	key0	16	test.t1.i,test.t1.j	1	100.00	
2	DERIVED	t1	ALL	NULL	NULL	NULL	NULL	10	100.00	Using where
Warnings:
Note	1003	/* select#1 */ select /*+ NO_MERGE(`dt2`@`select#1`) NO_MERGE(`dt`@`select#1`) */ `dt`.`a` AS `a`,`dt`.`b` AS `b`,`test`.`t1`.`i` AS `a`,`test`.`t1`.`j` AS `b` from (/* select#2 */ select `test`.`t1`.`i` AS `a`,`test`.`t1`.`j` AS `b` from `test`.`t1` where `test`.`t1`.`i` < 3 and `test`.`t1`.`j` > 8) `dt` join `test`.`t1` where `dt`.`a` = `test`.`t1`.`i` and `dt`.`b` = `test`.`t1`.`j` and `test`.`t1`.`i` < 3 and `test`.`t1`.`j` > 8
{code}
We have `Opt_hints_qb` object which has two children of Type `Opt_hints_table` (for `dt` and `dt2`).
This function will be called first for `dt` and will set Opt_hints_qb::fixed to DELAYED (note: the whole query block but not this particular table `dt`):
{code:cpp}
void Opt_hints_qb::fix_hints_for_table(TABLE_LIST *table_list)
{
  Opt_hints_table *tab= static_cast<Opt_hints_table *>(find_by_name(table_list->alias));

  DBUG_ASSERT(!table_list->opt_hints_table);

  if (is_delayed())
    return;
  ...
  set_fixed(Opt_hints::Fixed_state::DELAYED);
}
{code}

The next call to this function for `dt2` will skip the logic upon checking `is_delayed()`. This may be the cause of the hint ignorance, probably.

Something similar is observed at setup_tables():
{code:cpp}
      if (qb_hints &&                              // (1)
          ((!table_list->opt_hints_table &&        // (2)
            !(select_insert && is_insert_part)) || // (3)
           qb_hints->is_delayed()))                // (4)
      {
        table_list->opt_hints_table=
            qb_hints->fix_hints_for_table(table_list->table,
                                          table_list->alias);
      }
{code}
Note that we are inside a loop iterating through all TABLE_LISTs of a query block. So why do we check `qb_hints->is_delayed()` while qb_hints contains more than one table and some of them may be fixed while others delayed?

There may be even more complications when delayed-style hints are intermingled with traditional ones in a single query block. 

So I think a more viable approach would be setting and analyzing such `delayed` flag at the table level (for example, for `Opt_hints_table`).

I would also like to explore the possibility of working with hints set for TABLE_LIST's instead of TABLE's (and inviting you to join). This could save us from all those complications with delayed fixing.  From the first impressions TABLE's are only required for index-level hints as they keep `keyinfo` structures. Other hints seem to be fine with TABLE_LISTs.

items.toString:
When a query filters results with a WHERE condition, that query may be more efficient when the optimizer pushes all or part of the WHERE condition down into a derived table, if a derived table is indeed present in the query.  The DERIVED_CONDITION_PUSHDOWN hint explicitly tells the optimizer to apply this optimization, even if the condition_pushdown_for_derived optimizer switch is off. Conversely, NO_DERIVED_CONDITION_PUSHDOWN lets you turn it off for specific queries.

Derived tables can sometimes be "merged" into the main query, essentially treating them as if they were part of the original table. This can lead to more efficient execution plans.  For example, 
SELECT a+2 FROM (select b*2+1 as a from t1) t; 
will be merged such that the derived table t is removed and the selected columns recombined, resulting in
SELECT b*2+3 FROM t1;
The MERGE hint forces this behavior, while NO_MERGE prevents it.

