      vcol_field->table->covering_vcol_keys.intersect(vcol_field->part_of_key);
      vcol_field->table->covering_vcol_keys_to_merge= true;
    }
  }
  while ((tl= ti++))
  {
    if (tl->table && tl->table->covering_vcol_keys_to_merge)
    {
      tl->table->covering_keys.merge(tl->table->covering_vcol_keys);
      tl->table->covering_vcol_keys_to_merge= false;
    }
  }
{code}

Symbolically, the question is:

Given the values of (x1 n x2 n ... n xn), y1, y2, ..., yn, find the value of (x1 u y1) n (x2 u y2) n ... n (xn u yn), where n stands for intersects and u for union, x1, ..., xn are the part_of_key of the n select items without considering vcol indexes, and y1, ..., yn are part_of_key of the n select items attributed to vcol indexes.

In the example above, n = 2, y1 = \{\}, y2 = \{0\}, so the question reduces to x1 n (x2 u \{0\}) = (x1 n x2) u (x1 n \{0\}). So in this case we do need the value of x1, that is, we need to recover / recalculate part_of_key of the first select item when we have the necessary information of vcol expressions.

I was able to fix the above wrong result with this amended idea:

Add a new function {{update_vcol_key_covering()}}, called from {{substitute_indexed_vcols_for_join()}}, which does the following:

For each vcol field, update its part_of_key by merging it with the intersection of field->part_of_key for field in the tree of vcol->expr

Then redo the calculation of table key coverings in the following way:

{noformat}
for each select item:
  if item is the expr of a vcol_field:
    vcol_field->table->covering_keys.intersect(vcol_field->part_of_key)
  else:
    walk the item tree and update the table key coverings as usual
{noformat}

