The failure above is caused by:

1. At optimization stage, the index c is chosen for vc
2. JT_NEXT plan is chosen, as well as keyread
3. At exec stage, TABLE::update_virtual_fields() does not compute the values of vc, because keyread is on

One could try to tweak the find_shortest_key() to try to use the vc index, but that would not help with say {{select vc from t force index (c);}}.

To fix this issue, I relaxed the conditions in TABLE::update_virtual_fields(), so that the vcol values are updated if the chosen index is one of the new ones introduced in the patch, i.e. an index that does not have the vcol as a part, but its leaves.

I introduced a Field::vcol_part_of_key, a bitmap of the "original" index covering for a vcol field, i.e. one that has vcol as a part. This way we can check the "non-conventional" chosen index by checking whether it is in vcol->part_of_key but not int vcol->vcol_part_of_key.

I also set the vcol in the read_set when this happens, to avoid assertion failures of marked_for_read().

