https://jira.mariadb.org/browse/MDEV-36132?focusedCommentId=305270&page=com.atlassian.jira.plugin.system.issuetabpanels:comment-tabpanel#comment-305270   
Denote this  CoveringKeysNotUpdatedAfterRewrite. 

I was wondering if we could recompute table->covering_keys after we've done the rewrite. 

Normally, table->covering_keys is updated inside fix_fields():
{code}
  #0  Bitmap<64u>::intersect 
  #1  in update_field_dependencies 
  #2  in find_field_in_table 
  #3  in find_field_in_table_ref 
  #4  in find_field_in_tables 
  #5  in Item_field::fix_fields 
...
{code}

There is  SELECT_LEX::update_used_tables()  which updates many attributes.  But it doesn't update {{table->covering_keys}}. 
even if  class Item_field has:

{code:cpp}
  void update_used_tables() override
  {
    update_table_bitmaps();
  }
  void update_table_bitmaps()
  {
    if (field && field->table)
    {
      TABLE *tab= field->table;
      tab->covering_keys.intersect(field->part_of_key);
      if (tab->read_set)
        tab->mark_column_with_deps(field);
    }
  }
{code}

... wait, actually it does try to update covering_keys in some cases. Look at this (in SELECT_LEX::update_used_tables):
{code:cpp}
          TABLE *tab= tl->table;
          tab->covering_keys= tab->s->keys_for_keyread;
          tab->covering_keys.intersect(tab->keys_in_use_for_query);
          /*
            View/derived was merged. Need to recalculate read_set
            bitmaps here. For example:
{code}

(TODO: so we can just reset tab->covering_fix and this problem will be resolved?)

Also, there is another way: Item_field::update_table_bitmaps_processor.  It is used by some subquery code (I wonder why) and was introduced here:
https://github.com/mariadb/server/commit/fdf789a7eadf864ecc0e617f25f795fafda55026
https://github.com/mariadb/server/commit/945f12cf8fc361844f3553599795beb549fc8c1d

The second piece of input: Denote this  *ShouldRewriteModifyAllFields*:

When find_order_in_list() resolves ORDER BY clause it does this:
{code:cpp}
    if (order_item->fix_fields_if_needed_for_order_by(thd, order->item) ||
        thd->is_error())
      return TRUE; /* Wrong field. */
   ...
    order_item= *order->item; // Item can change during fix_fields()                                                          
    all_fields.push_front(order_item, thd->mem_root);
    ref_pointer_array[el]= order_item;
   ...
    order->item= &ref_pointer_array[el];
{code}

Later on, we will call:
{code:cpp}
        subst_vcol_if_compatible(ctx, NULL, order->item, vcol_field);
{code}
This will change  {{*(order->item)}}, that is, it will change {{ref_pointer_array[N]}}. 

But {{JOIN::all_fields}} will still have the pointer to the item before the rewrite.  Is this OK?

Regarding CoveringKeysNotUpdatedAfterRewrite, updating table covering keys after substitution in optimization was considered earlier and rejected. The problem is that the table covering keys have to be recomputed from scratch. The reason was explained in my comment 3 weeks ago:

https://jira.mariadb.org/browse/MDEV-36132?focusedCommentId=304562&page=com.atlassian.jira.plugin.system.issuetabpanels%3Acomment-tabpanel#comment-304562

To rephrase, it is easy to remove from "ingredients" of table covering keys, but hard to add. Let x be covering keys of a table, and y1, y2, ... yk be covering keys of each item, and denote by "n" the intersecting operator, then

x = y1 n y2 n ... n yk

If an optimization step finds that one of the keys attributed to an item cannot be used, e.g. y2 is reduced to a subset y2'. We can update x by intersecting it with y2'.

But if an optimization step expands instead of reducing covering keys of an item, e.g. y2 is updated to be a superset y2'', how do we update x without recomputing it from scratch?

Regarding the code snippets you cited I've only observed update_field_dependencies happening in JOIN::prepare where the table index coverings are initially computed, not optimization.

I am not familiar with the circumstance of tab->covering_keys computation in SELECT_LEX::update_used_tables, but these two lines look more like a reset than an update. It happens when view/derived tables are involved (inside an "if (embedding->is_view_or_derived())"). I would be surprised if it does non-trivial updates like the one we are contemplating.

----

Regarding ShouldRewriteModifyAllFields, it is similar to the issue that caused the GROUP BY part of this task to be non-trivial.

More specifically, like ORDER BY items, GROUP BY items are added to JOIN::all_fields in find_order_in_list() during prepare. They then get substituted in subst_vcol_if_compatible. If JOIN::all_fields is not updated to include the vcol field item, then later in optimization when create_tmp_table is called, only the old vcol expr item will be included in the tmp table, and subsequently the following assertion fails, because cur_group->item is the new vcol field item which has not been processed to use (be part of) the tmp table:

{code:c++}
// Create_tmp_table::finalize:
    for (; cur_group ; cur_group= cur_group->next, m_key_part_info++)
    {
      Field *field=(*cur_group->item)->get_tmp_table_field();
      DBUG_ASSERT(field->table == table);
{code}

To fix this we can push_front the new vcol field item to all_fields during the substitution and increment tmp_table_param.field_count, see bb-12.1-mdev-36132-wip 13565687474190ffba2e5036236828e3de54780a mentioned in my previous comment

https://jira.mariadb.org/browse/MDEV-36132?focusedCommentId=305358&page=com.atlassian.jira.plugin.system.issuetabpanels:comment-tabpanel#comment-305358

But then there would still be a mismatch between ref_pointer_array and all_fields at the vcol expr if they are supposed to be aligned.

Also if we do try to include the new vcol field item we also need to consider deduplication, because find_order_in_list does try to avoid pushing duplicate items to all_fields (see code block below). So if there is already a vcol field item before the substitution, it would save space to try to follow the deduplication too, although I am not sure if it would cause any bugs if no deduplication is done.

{code:c++}
// find_order_in_list:
    if (from_field == not_found_field ||
        (from_field != view_ref_found ?
         /* it is field of base table => check that fields are same */
         ((*select_item)->type() == Item::FIELD_ITEM &&
          ((Item_field*) (*select_item))->field->eq(from_field)) :
         /*
           in is field of view table => check that references on translation
           table are same
         */
         ((*select_item)->type() == Item::REF_ITEM &&
          view_ref->type() == Item::REF_ITEM &&
          ((Item_ref *) (*select_item))->ref ==
          ((Item_ref *) view_ref)->ref)))
    {
      /*
        If there is no such field in the FROM clause, or it is the same field
        as the one found in the SELECT clause, then use the Item created for
        the SELECT field. As a result if there was a derived field that
        'shadowed' a table field with the same name, the table field will be
        chosen over the derived field.
      */
      order->item= &ref_pointer_array[counter];
      order->in_field_list=1;
      return FALSE;
{code}

[~psergei] I suppose one question would be: are there other optimizations that substitute ORDER BY or GROUP BY items during optimization? If so then we can study how they fix this problem, and if not then this could be another reason to do the substitution early, before the construction of JOIN::all_fields in setup_order. This could fix both CoveringKeysNotUpdatedAfterRewrite and ShouldRewriteModifyAllFields.

