*Auto-detection.* With the following patch:
{code:diff}
@@ -309,11 +347,11 @@ struct FVector::distance_greater_than()
     float k = scale * other->scale;
     float dp= dot_product(dims, other->dims, subdist_part);
     float subdist= (subabs2 + other->subabs2 - k * dp)/subdist_part*vec_len;
-    if (subdist > than*subdist_margin)
-      return subdist;
     dp+= dot_product(dims+subdist_part, other->dims+subdist_part,
                      vec_len - subdist_part);
-    return abs2 + other->abs2 - k * dp;
+    float dist= abs2 + other->abs2 - k * dp;
+    stats.add(subdist/dist);
+    return dist;
   }
 };
{code}
one can clearly see  ([^stats.ods]) that applicable (matryoshka, randomized) and non-applicable datasets can be very reliably differentiated by looking at the standard deviation of predicted/actual distance ratio.

MTR missing coverage as per 156cc99ee1aadd2d54a39f864ff948f7515f45cf, initially obtained with nm big + ps big + galera big, but apparently it's the same with just {{perl ./mtr --do-test="vector*"}}
(note that I only ran it on x86_64, maybe it would be reduced slightly on all platforms where we run MTR, but probably not by much -- by the look of it, most of the missed lines are platform-independent).

{noformat:title=MTR missing coverage for the patch from the baseline 6a2afb42ba86188ccda0972f9c2df363f34e10a0 -- the feature + cleanups}
Lines with zero coverage (32/282):

