In the new test combination, we the following predicate (which is duplicating an atomic relaxed load of {{enabled}} for no reason) wrongly returns {{false}}:
{code:c++}
  /** Check if ahi is enable for an index
      @return true if enabled */
  inline bool is_enabled(const dict_index_t *index) const noexcept
  {
    /*
       Index is enabled if global ahi is enabled and index can be enabled.
       If enabled is set to 2 (IF_SPECIFIED), only enable indexes declared
       with ahi enabled on (ahi_enabled == 2).
       We don't have to check if index->search_info.ahi_enabled != 0
       as the test enabled <= ahi_enabled will not be true in this case.
    */
    return (unlikely(enabled != 0) &&
            enabled <= index->search_info.ahi_enabled);
  }
{code}
The {{return false}} from the above predicate is contradicting the following claim:
{quote}Having the ADAPTIVE_HASH_INDEX TABLE option set to NO will disable adaptive hash index for all indexes in the table that does not have the index option adaptive_hash_index=yes.{quote}
The reason for this return value is that {{2<=1}} does not hold. The value 1 was assigned to {{index->search_info.ahi_enabled}} via a local variable {{ahi}}, which had been copied in the following:
{code:c++|title=d52fe8f7e7241d1f780f40eba39f4679b8a7a8f6:storage/innobase/handler/ha_innodb.cc|linenumbers|firstline=6084}
                if (table->key_info[i].option_struct->adaptive_hash_index == 0 &&
                    ib_table->ahi_enabled)
                  ahi= ib_table->ahi_enabled;    // 1 (default) or 2 (forced)
{code}
The table attribute was initialized to 1 instead of 2 in the following:
{code:c++|title=d52fe8f7e7241d1f780f40eba39f4679b8a7a8f6:storage/innobase/handler/ha_innodb.cc|linenumbers|firstline=2990}
  if (!table_share->option_struct ||
      table_share->option_struct->adaptive_hash_index == 0)
    innodb_table->ahi_enabled= 1;               // Not defined, use default ahi setting
{code}
Besides, I believe that the functions {{ha_innobase::open()}} and {{innobase_copy_frm_flags_from_table_share()}} are the wrong place to initialize these data fields. A more correct place would be {{innodb_copy_stat_flags()}}, synchronized with the initialization of the {{STATS_PERSISTENT}} and {{STATS_AUTO_RECALC}} attributes. There, the initialization is properly protected against any race conditions.

