UPDATE ON [2025-08-13 Wed]: CI reported failures in a few main suite tests.

Some testcases, similar to the description, and none of these use unordered index scan:

{code:sql}
--source include/have_partition.inc
--source include/have_sequence.inc

CREATE TABLE t1 (
  c int,
  d INT,
  INDEX idx1(c, d)
) PARTITION BY RANGE COLUMNS(d) (
 PARTITION `p1` VALUES LESS THAN (10),
 PARTITION `p2` VALUES LESS THAN (20),
 PARTITION `p3` VALUES LESS THAN (30),
 PARTITION `p4` VALUES LESS THAN (40),
 PARTITION `p5` VALUES LESS THAN MAXVALUE
);

insert into t1 select t2.seq, t3.seq from seq_100_to_200 t2, seq_1_to_50 t3;

explain select * from t1 where c = 120 ORDER BY d DESC LIMIT 1;
select * from t1 where c = 120 ORDER BY d DESC LIMIT 2;
select * from t1 where c = 120 ORDER BY d LIMIT 2;
select * from t1 where c = 120 LIMIT 2;

drop table t1;
{code}

For starters, It would make sense to implement for ASC ordering which should be easier. UPDATE on [2025-08-13 Wed]: in the optimizer meeting yesterday [~psergei] said he expects DESC to be very easy once ASC is implemented.

For the proposal to work, the ORDER BY expression and the PARTITION BY RANGE expression had better be identical, otherwise it could be equivalent to sort by a column PARTITIONed BY HASH:

{code:sql}
CREATE TABLE t1 (
  c int,
  d INT,
  INDEX idx1(c, d)
) PARTITION BY RANGE (d % 5) (
 PARTITION `p1` VALUES LESS THAN (1),
 PARTITION `p2` VALUES LESS THAN (2),
 PARTITION `p3` VALUES LESS THAN (3),
 PARTITION `p4` VALUES LESS THAN (4),
 PARTITION `p5` VALUES LESS THAN MAXVALUE
);

insert into t1 select t2.seq, t3.seq from seq_100_to_200 t2, seq_1_to_50 t3;

# should use priority queue
select * from t1 where c = 120 ORDER BY d LIMIT 2;
# no need for priority queue
select * from t1 where c = 120 ORDER BY d % 5 LIMIT 2;
{code}

The current condition for using unordered index scan is rather strict. The likely scenario is that an unordered scan is applied to a table with more than one partition to read only if the table is not the first in the join order (after join optimization):

{code:c++}
/* in make_join_readinfo: */
  for (tab= first_tab= first_linear_tab(join,
                                        WITH_BUSH_ROOTS, WITHOUT_CONST_TABLES);
       tab;
       tab= next_linear_tab(join, tab, WITH_BUSH_ROOTS))
  {
//  [... 11 lines elided]
    tab->sorted= sorted;
    sorted= 0;                                  // only first must be sorted
//  [... 188 lines elided]
  }
...
/* in ha_partition::index_init: */
  m_ordered= sorted;
...
/* in ha_partition::partition_scan_set_up: */
  if (m_part_spec.start_part == m_part_spec.end_part)
//  [... 9 lines elided]
  else
  {
//  [... 15 lines elided]
    m_ordered_scan_ongoing= m_ordered;
  }
{code}

For ASC vs DESC, note that:

First, ordered scan is forced on DESC

{code:c++}
/* in ha_partition::common_index_read: */
  if (have_start_key &&
      (m_start_key.flag == HA_READ_PREFIX_LAST ||
       m_start_key.flag == HA_READ_PREFIX_LAST_OR_PREV ||
       m_start_key.flag == HA_READ_BEFORE_KEY))
  {
    reverse_order= TRUE;
    m_ordered_scan_ongoing= TRUE;
  }
{code}

Second, there is handle_ordered_prev, but no "unordered prev" method, and also index_prev always uses ordered scan:

{code:c++}
int ha_partition::index_prev(uchar * buf)
{
  DBUG_ENTER("ha_partition::index_prev");
  decrement_statistics(&SSV::ha_read_prev_count);

  /* TODO: read comment in index_next */
  if (m_index_scan_type == partition_index_first)
    DBUG_RETURN(HA_ERR_WRONG_COMMAND);
  DBUG_RETURN(handle_ordered_prev(buf));
}
{code}

Implemented a PoC which sets m_ordered_scan_ongoing to false when the PARTITION BY RANGE item is identical to the ORDER BY item:

{noformat}
95bda29724d bb-main-mdev-37330 MDEV-37330 [poc] Set to use unordered index scan when PARTITION BY RANGE item is identical to ORDER BY item
{noformat}

