comments.body:
So far, I have implemented the logic for switching between the two formats when executing the following statements while the server is processing some workload:
{code:sql}
SET GLOBAL innodb_log_archive=ON;
SET GLOBAL innodb_log_archive=OFF;
{code}
These statements should be correctly interlocked with respect to {{SET GLOBAL innodb_log_file_size}}, which will take immediate effect when {{innodb_log_archive=ON}} (specify the size of future log files), instead of invoking the MDEV-27812 log-rebuiding logic that we retain for {{innodb_log_archive=OFF}}.

An in-place update between these two formats has been implemented. When a file is named {{ib_logfile0}} (to indicate the old {{innodb_log_archive=OFF}} format), it must always be in that format. During a transition between the two file header formats, the file will always be named like {{ib_%016x.log}}. This should simplify crash recovery (which has not been implemented yet).

Currently, the {{innodb_encrypt_log}} format cannot be identified or represented. I have been thinking of using a different file name pattern, such as {{ib_%016x.enc}}. This should be a fairly trivial change.

Another omission of crash recovery is that we currently always start from the very beginning of the first archived log file (instead of the latest available checkpoint, or the checkpoint specified by the new parameter {{innodb_log_recovery_start}}). The new parameters {{innodb_log_recovery_start}} and {{innodb_log_recovery_target}} are not being fully validated either. Because no switchover to the next archived log file has been implemented, about a third of the regression test suite would fail due to data directory corruption.

In the modified test {{innodb.log_file_size_online}} we got [an assertion failure about potential buffer overflow|https://buildbot.mariadb.org/#/builders/866/builds/1648], which I have been unable to reproduce locally so far:
{noformat:title=MDEV-37949 6680d2adbe4d95141032d0b43a9e7548f180f99b}
innodb.log_file_size_online 'slow'       w39 [ fail ]
        Test ended at 2025-12-22 14:25:10
CURRENT_TEST: innodb.log_file_size_online
mysqltest: At line 60: query 'SET GLOBAL innodb_log_archive=OFF' failed: <Unknown> (2013): Lost connection to server during query
…
mariadbd: /home/buildbot/src/storage/innobase/mtr/mtr0mtr.cc:1301: static void log_t::append(byte *&, const void *, size_t): Assertion `log_sys.is_mmap() ? ((d >= log_sys.buf && d + size <= log_sys.buf + log_sys.file_size) || (log_sys.archive && d >= log_sys.resize_buf && d + size <= log_sys.resize_buf + log_sys.resize_target)) : (d >= log_sys.buf && d + size <= log_sys.buf + log_sys.buf_size)' failed.
{noformat}
I only have {{clang-21}} available on my local system, and MDEV-38419 prevents me from using the same build options. I believe that this race condition should be easily reproducible once we are ready to move to the stress testing phase, that is, after the crash recovery is feature-complete.

The following now passes the default test suites for me:
{code:sh}
mysql-test/mtr --parallel=auto --force --big-test --mysqld=--loose-innodb-log-archive --skip-test=mariabackup
{code}
I squashed and rebased this to a single commit. There are a few items left, including augmenting the 2-file recovery to multi-file recovery. Come to think of it, normal crash recovery should never involve multiple files, because we would start from the latest available checkpoint by defeault. Multi-file recovery can be exercised by specifying a smaller {{innodb_log_recovery_start}}; this is what restoring an incremental backup in MDEV-14992 would involve.

I am testing multi-file recovery by invoking the existing regression test suite with the following additional parameters, basically forcing the write-ahead log to be always replayed from the very beginning (database initialization):
{code:bash}
mysql-test/mtr --parallel=auto --mysqld=--loose-innodb-log-{recovery-start=12288,archive}
{code}
Some observations:
* Any test that sets {{innodb_encrypt_log=ON}} will be skipped, because those tests would initially bootstrap the server with {{innodb_encrypt_log=OFF}}, and we can’t change the format while {{innodb_log_archive=ON}} is in effect.
* Any test that sets {{innodb_read_only=ON}} must override {{innodb_log_recovery_start=0}} in order to avoid an error that {{innodb_read_only}} prevents crash recovery.
* Changing {{innodb_undo_tablespaces}} will make a recovery attempt with an earlier {{innodb_log_recovery_start}} fail due to there being no {{FILE_MODIFY}} records for the now-unknown undo tablespaces. (Recovery determines the undo tablespaces identifiers by reading the {{undo*}} files directly. For any other modified tablespaces than the system tablespace and the undo tablespace, there must exist {{FILE_MODIFY}} records.)
* A recovery attempt with an earlier {{innodb_log_recovery_start}} than a system tablespace {{autoshrink}} may warn about doublewrite buffer entries that point past the end of the system tablespace.

Today, I believe that I got the multi-file recovery to work in the memory-mapped code path. For the {{pread}} based code path, there are a few failing tests, which I will analyze and fix tomorrow:
{code:bash}
mysql-test/mtr --parallel=auto --force --big-test --max-test-fail=0 --mysqld=--loose-innodb-log-{recovery-start=12288,archive,file-mmap=OFF} --skip-test=mariabackup
{code}
{noformat}
Completed: Failed 34/5702 tests, 99.39% were successful.

Failing test(s): encryption.innodb-page_encryption-32k innodb.insert_into_empty innodb.innodb-bigblob innodb.innodb_bulk_create_index_debug innodb_zip.wl5522_debug_zip innodb.alter_copy_bulk innodb.sys_truncate_debug innodb.bulk_load innodb.sys_truncate_shutdown_debug innodb.sys_truncate_large innodb.restart innodb.innodb-index innodb_gis.rtree_split innodb_fts.innodb_fts_stopword_charset sql_sequence.read_only main.mdev-35046 innodb.innodb_28867993 innodb.innodb_bug59641 innodb.innodb_force_recovery_rollback innodb_gis.rtree_drop_index innodb.alter_crash_rebuild innodb_gis.kill_server
{noformat}
In addition to fixing these test failures, I must write a test and a proper implementation of crash recovery during {{SET GLOBAL innodb_log_archive}}.

To facilitate further performance testing, I [merged some recent changes to 12.3|https://github.com/MariaDB/server/commit/02aedc106596fe494b4fa408a627081319fbecad] and [merged the 11.4 based MDEV-37949 branch on top|https://github.com/MariaDB/server/commit/5b5c71fed60899b3aced97cb2b66c7d082853a52]. The following tests that do not use {{mariadb-backup}} (which does not support the {{innodb_log_archive=ON}} format) would fail when testing some nonstandard parameters:
{code:bash|title=st-12.3-MDEV-37949}
mysql-test/mtr --parallel=auto --big-test --force --mysqld=--loose-innodb-log-{archive,file-size=4m,recovery-start=12288} binlog.binlog_recover_async_rollback_trx binlog_in_engine.recovery_no_prealloc innodb.innodb_buffer_pool_load_now innodb.row_format_redundant main.flush_read_lock
{code}
There is a known design issue MDEV-39162 that affects performance when setting {{innodb_log_archive=ON}}.

Important limitation (we should mention in the docs): implicit query block names are not supported for DML operations. If someone wants to apply a hint, for example:
{code:sql}
UPDATE /*+ NO_RANGE_OPTIMIZATION(t2@dt)*/ t3,
    (SELECT t1.* FROM t1, t2 WHERE t1.f1 > t2.f1) AS dt
SET t3.f3 = 'mnbv' WHERE t3.f1 = dt.f1;
{code}
they should use explicit query block names:
{code:sql}
UPDATE /*+ NO_RANGE_OPTIMIZATION(t2@dt)*/ t3,
    (SELECT /*+ QB_NAME(dt) */ t1.* FROM t1, t2 WHERE t1.f1 > t2.f1) AS dt
SET t3.f3 = 'mnbv' WHERE t3.f1 = dt.f1;
{code}

P.S. Implementing a specific warning for this case turned out to be not trivial, because it's hard to distinguish between attempts of using _implicit_ QB names and an _incorrect_ (missing) ones.

h4. (Outdated! the solution mentioned in the comment below avoids complications of two-stage hint resolution) 
h5. Rationale for splitting hints resolution into early and late stages.

Basically, all hints except MERGE and NO_MERGE hints can be resolved after `open_tables()`, i.e. in late stage. However, MERGE and NO_MERGE hints have to be resolved before `open_tables()` because they are applied at `TABLE_LIST::init_derived()` which is called during `open_tables()`. The decision whether to merge or to materialize a derived table is made there:
{code:title=TABLE_LIST::init_derived()}
   bool is_derived_merge_allowed=
        hint_table_state(thd, this, MERGE_HINT_ENUM,
            optimizer_flag(thd, OPTIMIZER_SWITCH_DERIVED_MERGE));
{code}
That is why resolving of MERGE and NO_MERGE hints is done in early stage. 

QB_NAME hints also have to be resolved before MERGE/NO_MERGE to make sure statements like this work:
{code:sql}
SELECT /*+ QB_NAME(foo) NO_MERGE(@`foo`) */ e.emp_name, s.salary FROM (
select * from employees) e JOIN  (select * from salaries) s ON e.emp_id = s.emp_id;
{code}

In fact, `TABLE_LIST::init_derived` is called twice: first time during `open_tables()` and second time during `JOIN::prepare() -> .. mysql_derived_prepare()`. It seems to be enough to apply NO_MERGE hint only during the second call. That means NO_MERGE hint can be resolved in late stage along with all other hints. However, that does not apply to MERGE hint. 
If there is a way to successfully apply MERGE hint after `open_tables()`, all hints can be resolved in late stage, thus the introduced complexity of two-stage hint resolution can be removed.

items.fromString:
*Idea*
Every view and derived table participating in a query gets an implicit query block name based on its name or alias. Optimizer hints can be applied to internal objects of view/derived tables reference this implicit query block name.

*Example*
{code:sql}
select * from (select * from t1 where a > 10) as DT;   -- `DT` gets an implicit query block name `qb_name_dt`

create view v1 as select * from t1 where a > 10 and b > 100;
select * from v1 as vvv, t2 where vvv.a = t2.a;  -- view `vvv` gets an implicit query block name `qb_name_vvv`
{code}
(Implicit names may have other forms, e.g. `qb_vvv` or `qb__vvv` (double underscore to avoid conflict with user-provided names). To be discussed)

*Benefits*
Objects inside derived tables and views can be referenced by optimizer hints without explicit addition of the `QB_NAME()` hint:
{code:sql}
select /*+ no_index(t1@qb_name_t2) */ * from (select * from t1 where a > 10) as t2;

select /*+ index_merge(t1@qb_name_v1 idx_a,idx_b) */ * from v1, t2 where v1.a = t2.a;
{code}

items.from:
Another limitation: QB_NAME with path does not work in conjunction with MERGE/NO_MERGE hints:
{code:sql}
EXPLAIN EXTENDED
select /*+ QB_NAME(qb4, dw .dv .du .dt)  NO_MERGE(@qb4) */ emp_id from (
select emp_id from (
   select emp_id from (
     select emp_id from (
        select emp_id from employees) dt
       )  du
    ) dv
) dw;
id	select_type	table	type	possible_keys	key	key_len	ref	rows	filtered	Extra
1	SIMPLE	employees	system	NULL	NULL	NULL	NULL	0	0.00	Const row not found
Warnings:
Warning	4220	Query block name `qb4` is not found for NO_MERGE hint
{code}

This is caused by the fact that QB_NAME with path can only be resolved in late stage, after `open_tables()` when the tree of views/derived tables/tables is built. At the same time, MERGE/NO_MERGE hints are resolved in early stage (see the comment above). So, by the time of NO_MERGE hint resolution in the example above QB_NAME hint is not yet resolved, so `qb4` is undefined.

comments.body:
Something I noticed while working at MDEV-9826: the MD5() SQL function does not factor in collation, so two strings may be equal but hash to different results (see example). That is different from the PARTITION BY KEY hash requirements.

{code:sql}
create table t1 (a varchar(40));
insert into t1 values (' '), ('                               ');
select * from t1 where a = ' ';
a


select a, md5(a) from t1;
a	md5(a)
 	7215ee9c7d9dc229d2921a40e899ec5f
                               	6246e5168084285e5e093f9ef616f5b2
{code}

I think that we must provide both 1-ary and 2-ary variants of these functions, so that the hash values can be computed piecewise, for example in a {{GROUP_CONCAT}} expression. After all, the XXH functions do expose a parameter for an initial value.

MDEV-27208 introduced a 2-ary variant of our built-in {{CRC32()}} function. In the test {{main.func_math}} you can see that the following expressions return the same result:
{code:sql}
SELECT CRC32(CRC32('MySQL'),''),CRC32(CRC32('My'),'SQL'),CRC32(0,'MySQL');
{code}
It would be ideal if for example {{XXH32('MariaDB')}} returned the same value as {{XXH32(0,'MariaDB')}}.

fields.description:
The return value for Type_handler::Item_save_in_value() is misleading. It currently returns:
- true if the value evaluated to SQL NULL
- false otherwise

Similar functions return true if some error happened. So the code looks confusing.

Let's change the return type from bool to void.

Let's also add a new method st_value::is_null(), to make SQL NULL tests simpler, as follows:

{code:cpp}
  bool is_null() const
  {
    return m_type == DYN_COL_NULL;
  }
{code}

so the callers, instead of testing the return value, can test value.is_null().

The caller code will change, for example, like this:

{code:cpp}
   st_value tmp;
-  if (!item->save_in_value(thd, &tmp))
+  item->save_in_value(thd, &tmp);
+  if (!tmp.is_null())
{code}

