comments.body:
It seems the {{btr_cur_need_opposite_intention()}} function is being called at the root level, which has no siblings indeed: this could explain the high count of {{nosib}} in the testing which causes the function to return {{true}}.
I'll try to reproduce and verify the behavior.
In {{btr_cur_t::search_leaf()}}:


{code:c++}
  switch (latch_mode) {
  default:
    break;
  case BTR_MODIFY_TREE:
    if (btr_cur_need_opposite_intention(block->page, index()->is_clust(),
                                        lock_intention,
                                        node_ptr_max_size, compress_limit,
                                        page_cur.rec))
      /* If the rec is the first or last in the page for pessimistic
      delete intention, it might cause node_ptr insert for the upper
      level. We should change the intention and retry. */
    need_opposite_intention:
      return pessimistic_search_leaf(tuple, mode, mtr);
{code}

If this is called for the root, there is no "upper level" to refer to.      
Possibly the root node needs dedicated handling.

[~Steve Shaw] could you please verify the behavior of https://github.com/iMineLink/server/commit/37e649f9b56cbe0b06d0bc15c953a0ab5e11a679 under HammerDB?
{{btr_cur_need_opposite_intention()}} has been modified to return false when called on the root.
The stub commit contains also two new counters for monitoring the index lock upgrades and opposite intention check called on root:

{code:sql}
SHOW GLOBAL STATUS LIKE 'Innodb_btr_index_lock_upgrades';
SHOW GLOBAL STATUS LIKE 'Innodb_btr_need_opposite_intention_root';
{code}

By taking the difference between before and after the test is run, one can see how many upgrades were done and how many opposite intention check on root were performed.
I experienced the number of index lock upgrades being 60~40% the original one in both the {{index_lock_upgrade.test}} and a custom lua script for sysbench that performs updates that "expand" records previously with null values (similarly to what the original testing was doing).
Less index lock upgrades could lead to more parallelism and increased performance indeed.
As a note, I ran the mtr suite several times in Debug builds without incurring in any hang.
I will continue investigation to see if {{btr_cur_need_opposite_intention()}} can be further improved.

Thank you very much.

[~mdcallag], I appreciate your comment. I filed MDEV-40619 for an idea that got while thinking this through, to implement an optimistic lock escalation path for {{BTR_MODIFY_LEAF}} operations.

Hard-to-reproduce bugs like MDEV-15326, MDEV-32132, MDEV-29835 (all introduced by changes in MySQL 5.7) are a challenge. I believe that MySQL is affected by all of them, but I have no proof of that, because we do not extensively test competing products.

Related to this, I found some interesting analysis in the commit message of [Bug#39129182 Mysqld Crash due to "Deadlock Detected!" Error|https://github.com/mysql/mysql-server/commit/0ca050765605c5de8873616d32ee9f6adfd70421], which nicely explains the mechanism of predicting when a page split may be needed. I believe that we fixed the deadlock in MDEV-14637, MDEV-30400, and MDEV-29835. The downside of eliminating the low-probability deadlocks was that some escalated locking was introduced.

The MySQL change introduces a debug assertion that we are missing. In our current code base, I think that it should look like this:
{code:diff}
diff --git a/storage/innobase/btr/btr0cur.cc b/storage/innobase/btr/btr0cur.cc
index 6f9ad403860..7abaeaf35da 100644
--- a/storage/innobase/btr/btr0cur.cc
+++ b/storage/innobase/btr/btr0cur.cc
@@ -1467,6 +1467,7 @@ dberr_t btr_cur_t::search_leaf(const dtuple_t *tuple, page_cur_mode_t mode,
   offsets= rec_get_offsets(page_cur.rec, index(), offsets, 0, ULINT_UNDEFINED,
                            &heap);
 
+  ut_ad(rec_offs_size(offsets) <= node_ptr_max_size);
   ut_ad(block == mtr->at_savepoint(block_savepoint));
 
   switch (latch_mode) {
{code}
I noted in MDEV-40617 that our {{btr_node_ptr_max_size()}} fails to account for {{SPATIAL INDEX}}. (MDEV-40619 might eliminate the entire function.)

The {{SHRINK}} option that was introduced in this task gives the user a little more control (and, unfortunately, burden or responsibility). If you want to keep the B-trees small, then the costly page merges and splits are bound to occur more frequently, leading to write amplification. Allowing some slack ({{SHRINK=ON}}) will lead to space amplification and possibly cause a regression when the bloated index trees will no longer fit in the buffer pool.

bugfixes.MCOL-5987-recycle-oids, basic.MCOL-6293-partitions-table failures were caused by the following lines in the setup/regression_env_setup.test‎


{noformat}
--exec if test -f /etc/my.cnf.d/server.cnf; then sed -i 's/\[mysqld\]/\[mysqld\]\nlower_case_table_names=1/g' /etc/my.cnf.d/server.cnf; fi
--exec if test -f /etc/mysql/mariadb.conf.d/50.server.cnf; then sed -i 's/\[mysqld\]/\[mysqld\]\nlower_case_table_names=1/g' /etc/mysql/mariadb.conf.d/50-server.cnf; fi
{noformat}


It overrides the value of the lower_case_table_names variable set by the test script before running tests in case of /etc/mysql/mariadb.conf.d/50.server.cnf or /etc/my.cnf.d/server.cnf exists. It explains why failures are reproducible only with certain distributions (even certain iamages of the same distro).

