comments.body:
Branch `bb-13.1-pscan-test` represents the parallel execution of an InnoDB table split into chunks.

Test case to see in action:
{code:sql}
--source include/have_innodb.inc
--source include/have_sequence.inc

set session parallel_worker_threads=4;

create table t1 (a int , b int, c varchar(20), primary key(a))
  engine=innodb;

show create table t1;

--echo Insert 10000 records
insert into t1 select seq, seq, 'abcd' from seq_1_to_10000;

analyze format=json select * from t1;

drop table t1;
{code}

By changing the number of workers `set session parallel_worker_threads=4;` or commenting out this line one can compare the performance looking at  "r_total_time_ms" value in the ANALYZE output.  It's also possible change the number of inserted rows, for example raise it to 1 million: `insert into t1 select seq, seq, 'abcd' from seq_1_to_1000000`.

First results, unfortunately, don't look too exciting. Setting the number of workers to 1: `set session parallel_worker_threads=1`, i.e. just firing up the parallel execution framework without the actual parallelism causes 3 times slowdown compared to traditional single-threaded execution. 
Increasing the number of workers to 4-8 allows to compete or slightly improve the single-threaded performance (by 10-20%). Increasing further only slows down the execution.

Now it needs to figure out what is causing the slowdown observed.

Here's a question of what to name the new define that tags globals. Originally the name was {{RO_AFTER_INIT}}, but I found it ugly and confusing
{code:c}
RO_AFTER_INIT const char *wsrep_notify_cmd;
{code}
Like, what does it mean and why it's here on this variable?

The pushed commit uses
{code:c}
READ_ONLY_SYSVAR const char *wsrep_notify_cmd;
{code}
which clearly answers both questions in a self-documenting way. Drawback is — we might want to have other globals to be "read only after initialization" too, but they aren't "read-only sysvars" so the tag would look misleading and confusing again.

Suggestions?

I'm optimistic that Claude can write portable code, if you ask for it.  So please do ask for it, it will deliver. As it currently stands, it is again a GCC/ELF device, and needs some MSVC/PE treatment to work half-way portably.

