Yes, the first was fixed this morning, found by buildbot.

The third failed on Linux when I added tests ({{sys_var.sysvar_readlonly_debug}} — unfortunately, not run in buildbot on Windows), the wrong condition was {{!is_struct()}}, the intention was to skip struct variables, so negation was wrong. The second condition is correct. The logic there is, if there's, say,
{code:c}
char opt_value_buf[1024];
char *opt_value_ptr= opt_value_buf;
{code}
then both {{opt_value_ptr}} and {{opt_value_buf}} can be marked READ_ONLY_SYSVAR and there will be no need to copy the value to the new memroot, the buffer itself is in the {{ro_after_init}} section. We only need to copy strings that are not in the {{ro_after_init}} segment.

Other three — will do.

Question — yes. *pre*-cache would not make sense (in the scope of this MDEV), but caching and reusing THD between connections definitely would be good. As you've seen the implementation uses the fact that THDs are cached and preserves one allocated memroot page in case new connection will use uservars too.

